WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
The aldol reaction is a great reaction;

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.890
here we have two molecules of acetone

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.469
reacting in the presence of either acid or base

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:13.529
to form an intermediate hydroxyketone. The hydroxyketone can react further through an

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:19.499
elimination reaction to form an enone. In this process we get a new carbon–carbon bond and

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.939
The enone that we form can react in several places on the molecule.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.309
Whenever we see an enone we can think about it coming from the aldol reaction. To

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:35.579
analyze the starting materials that this enone has come from I like to draw a squiggly line over the double bond.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.570
The three carbons on the left containing the carbonyl comes from the side that formed the enolate and did the attacking.

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.730
That's why it still has its carbonyl.

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:49.049
The right-hand side came from being attacked in dehydration

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.890
and if we just think about replacing that squiggle with an oxygen

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:59.549
and getting back a carbonyl compound we can see that this molecule comes from two molecules of

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.149
acetone. This analysis is called retrosynthesis

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:09.059
and it's just a fancy term for thinking about a product and what starting materials it might have come from.

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:12.839
Now the aldol reaction of acetone with itself is a great reaction,

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.860
but it's a little boring.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.069
We just have two molecules of the same thing

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:23.279
reacting so we always get the same product—three carbons on either side make a new six carbon enone

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.890
But if we want to build up bigger, more complex

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:33.720
carbon skeletons, it would be really neat if we could react two different ketones and get more complex products.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:40.170
Reacting to different carbonyl compounds in an aldol reaction is called a crossed aldol and

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:46.949
sometimes this is possible under equilibrating conditions. Now remember when we treat a ketone with

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:52.559
hydroxide or an acid that each step of the aldol reaction is in equilibrium and

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.099
to do a cross alcohol under these conditions it requires

00:01:56.530 --> 00:02:02.280
special situations. First of all one of our carbonyl compounds can't be able to enolize.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:10.560
Secondly, that substrate that's not able to enolize should also be more reactive than the enolizable substrate.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.169
Okay, let's look at what this means with an example.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.399
We'll react benzaldehyde with acetone.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.430
If we look at this carbon to the left of the carbonyl, we can see it already has four bonds to carbon,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.939
thus it doesn't have any alpha hydrogens.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:34.410
On the other side of the carbonyl we have the aldehyde H—this is not an acidic proton and can't be deprotonated.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.309
Watch out for that. That's a common error I see with my students.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:44.370
So when this mixture is treated with sodium hydroxide, only acetone can enolize.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.510
The alpha hydrogen is deprotonated and we get our enolate.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.889
The enolate of acetone attacks benzaldehyde.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.939
Aldehydes are more reactive than ketones in part because they don't have an extra substituent on the carbonyl—

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.829
It's just a small H and due to sterics they can react much faster.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.729
Our intermediate alkoxide is protonated by water, which was produced when base

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.970
deprotonated to make the enolate in the first step. Now

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.749
I like to show hydroxide coming back in and

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.210
deprotonating to form another enolate

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:25.439
I'm forming the enolate here because it gives us an insight into the

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:29.550
reactivity we still have that resonance that makes that proton acidic.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:34.049
Now if we push arrows through the whole conjugated system

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.880
we can see how the hydroxide which isn't normally a good leaving group is

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.929
pushed out of the molecule because of that resonance stabilization

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.780
This is an E1CB

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.779
elimination and produces our enone.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:53.129
The E-alkene is favored because it is more stable.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.490
I mentioned that aldehydes are more reactive than ketones.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:04.050
Well, what about if our non-enolizable substrate is not more reactive than the enolizable substrate?

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:09.269
Let's do this reaction under acidic conditions. In this mechanism we'll first protonate

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.990
acetone. Water can come in to form the enol,

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.330
and

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:22.539
now the email of acetone has a hard time reacting with that very sterically hindered

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.040
diphenylketone.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:31.869
So instead it finds a less sterically hindered molecule of itself—of acetone— and it reacts.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:39.279
So, we don't get reaction with the substrate that we want, instead we get a self reaction predominating here.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:44.680
Okay, so what happens if both substrates have enolizablel protons?

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.440
Here we have two ketones—an unsymmetrical ketone and a symmetrical one.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.789
Each of these substrates can enolize and react with the other one.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:05:00.519
So if the enolate of the first compound reacts with the ketone of the second we'll get this product.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:07.820
Okay, so what if our symmetrical ketone enolizes?

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.900
This can react with our unsymmetrical ketone to give this product.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:22.160
But this isn't even the end of the story! We can think about our unsymmetrical ketone

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.820
enolizing and also reacting with a molecule of itself that will give this product that we'll call "C"

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:35.569
Finally, you guessed it—our other molecule can also react with itself so it enolizes,

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:40.459
reacts with the symmetrical ketone, and produces this product, "D."

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.670
What a mess!

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:49.309
Fortunately there is a solution and we can do crossed aldols under non-equilibrating conditions.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:53.809
Ok let's try to make product "A." For practice, we'll do our retrosynthesis.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.660
We're going to split down the bond.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.219
remember the side with the carbonyl is the one one that gets the negative charge,

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:03.890
forms the enolate, and

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:09.020
then on the lower portion of this molecule we add a carbonyl where the double bond is.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.879
Now we can see the starting materials we need and which one we need to enolize.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:21.470
Ok there's two methods to do this crossed aldol, and the first one involves using LDA—lithium diisopropylamide—

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.530
which looks like this.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.400
LDA is very sterically hindered because of those two

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:35.420
Isopropyl groups, so in the first step it's going to go for the more accessible, proton, which is the least sterically hindered.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:42.050
So it looks like it's giving us what we don't want—it's giving us the less substituted enolate here.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:48.619
However, we can control this by having a slight excess of our ketone with respect to LDA.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:55.100
The extra ketone we have in solution comes in and reacts with our kinetic enolate.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:07:02.240
The kinetic enolate is not as bulky, so over time it will form the thermodynamic enolate by

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:08.390
deprotonating at the carbon we want and will eventually form our more stable enolate.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:14.119
We can then add our second ketone into the reaction and get the product we want.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.799
Ok so let's show an overall reaction for how we would write this up.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:25.340
We'll start with our unsymmetrically- substituted ketone, and we want that in excess, so let's say we'll use

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.989
0.9 molar equivalents of LDA. In a second synthetic step,

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.689
we'll add in our symmetrical ketone,

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:40.219
and then if we work this reaction up with water we can actually sometimes isolate our hydroxy ketone compound. Here

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.639
we wanted the enone so we can treat this with a little bit of acid or base.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:50.419
For good measure we can warm the solution to promote elimination and get our enone out of our reaction.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:56.150
There's a second way to accomplish this reaction which I think is a little bit more straightforward.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:04.579
We can treat our unsymmetrically substituted ketone with triethylamine and chlorotrimethylsilane.

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.119
Triethylamine is not nearly as sterically hindered as

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:13.728
LDA so it can deprotonate the molecule forming our thermodynamic enolate

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:18.290
directly. In the next step oxygen attacks at silicon

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:24.020
displacing chlorine. I haven't shown a mechanism here cause this is likely a two-step process.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:29.599
That reaction forms a silyl enol ether which we can draw this way or

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:33.890
we can depict with this abbreviation where we show O-TMS.

00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:39.229
Silyl enol ethers are usually stable at room temperature, in

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:44.899
contrast to lithium enolates formed by LDA, which are only stable at cold temperatures.

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:53.030
Now we can react our pre-formed silyl enol ether with the ketone. This reaction requires titanium tetrachloride,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.940
which is a Lewis acid catalyst. It

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:00.770
coordinates to the oxygen of the ketone and helps the reaction to proceed.

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:06.739
Now one of the chlorine atoms from the titanium tetrachloride that was displaced

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:13.339
originally can come back and attack at silicon. Bear in mind this may be a two-step process, though

00:09:13.339 --> 00:09:17.689
I'm showing it attack and displace in one step here. If we work this up with water

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:25.310
we can usually isolate our hydroxy ketone. Further treatment with acid or base will give us our enone.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:31.369
Let's look at one more example of the crossed aldol. We'll do our typical retrosynthetic

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.300
analysis where we put a squiggly line over our double bond and

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.099
we can see that our enolate comes from the carbonyl side. Add a

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:46.969
carbonyl to the left-hand side and we can see it comes from these to starting materials; however, this time

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:50.480
we're using the kinetic enolate to do this reaction.

00:09:51.299 --> 00:09:53.719
Alright let's review our two methods for doing this.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:57.170
The first is LDA.

00:09:57.299 --> 00:09:58.559
remember in our last example

00:09:58.559 --> 00:10:05.359
we actually formed the kinetic enolate first then we let it equilibrate with a little bit of excess ketone.

00:10:05.939 --> 00:10:13.219
Well in this case we don't want that equilibration. So what we're going to use is a slight excess of LDA. That way

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:20.420
there's no ketone left behind and we can completely form the kinetic enolate. So let's sketch out our overall reaction. In the

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:24.589
first step use excess LDA—maybe 1.1 equivalents.

00:10:25.139 --> 00:10:31.039
Then we add in the ketone that we want attacked in the second step and since we're looking to dehydrate the

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:36.170
Intermediate hydroxyketone let's just work this up with acid and promote that dehydration.

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:45.730
Alright now let's look at the silyl enol ether method. We need to form the kinetic enolate and we can't actually do that with triethylamine.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:51.129
So we're going to start this process by using our slight excess of LDA.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.730
We generate our kinetic enolate completely

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.400
then react this with chloro-

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:04.660
trimethylsilane. The silyl enol ether can be reacted with the ketone in the presence of titanium tetrachloride.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:10.389
This gives us our intermediate and since we want to dehydrate this again

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:15.069
we're going to just add an acid to our work up and get out our enone.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:22.329
So in this lesson we've explored how crossed aldols can occur, sometimes under equilibrating conditions

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:24.400
but these conditions are really special.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:28.059
So we went over two multi-step processes

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:33.550
to form our enolates first and then react them with the desired ketone.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:41.080
This gives us the ability to do many different crossed aldol reactions that are not possible under equilibrating conditions

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:46.349
Because we can form many unique carbon skeletons, this is great for synthesis.

